  extend('c', '$', [
    { id: 'malloc', title: 'Dynamic memory', xp: 45, recap: '<code>malloc</code> asks for memory at runtime; always check for <code>NULL</code> and <code>free</code> it when done.', steps: [
      read('Memory on demand', `<p>Arrays need their size when you write the code. For “however many enemies this level has”, ask for memory while the program runs:</p>${pre('#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint count = 50;\nstruct Enemy *enemies = malloc(count * sizeof(struct Enemy));\nif (enemies == NULL) {\n    return 1;            // out of memory\n}\n/* … use enemies[0] … enemies[49] … */\nfree(enemies);           // give it back!')}${tip('Every <code>malloc</code> needs exactly one <code>free</code>. Forgetting it is a <b>memory leak</b>; doing it twice is a crash.')}`),
      quiz('What must you always do after you’re finished with malloc’d memory?', ['Nothing', 'Call free()', 'Set it to 0', 'Call malloc again'], 1),
      code({ task: 'Allocate an array of <code>count</code> ints with <code>malloc(count * sizeof(int))</code>, return 1 if it’s <code>NULL</code>, fill it so <code>scores[i] = i * 10</code>, then <code>free</code> it.', starter: '#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int count = 8;\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n', hints: ['<code>int *scores = malloc(count * sizeof(int));</code>', '<code>if (scores == NULL) return 1;</code>'], solution: '#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int count = 8;\n    int *scores = malloc(count * sizeof(int));\n    if (scores == NULL) return 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n        scores[i] = i * 10;\n    }\n    free(scores);\n    return 0;\n}\n', check: R([[/int\s*\*\s*scores\s*=\s*(\(\s*int\s*\*\s*\)\s*)?malloc\s*\(\s*count\s*\*\s*sizeof\s*\(\s*int\s*\)\s*\)/, 'Allocate with <code>int *scores = malloc(count * sizeof(int));</code>'], [/if\s*\(\s*(scores\s*==\s*NULL|!\s*scores)\s*\)/, 'Check <code>if (scores == NULL)</code>.'], [/scores\s*\[\s*i\s*\]\s*=\s*i\s*\*\s*10/, 'Fill it: <code>scores[i] = i * 10;</code>'], [/free\s*\(\s*scores\s*\)/, 'Free it with <code>free(scores);</code>']]), success: 'You’ve met the core of C: types, pointers and memory.' }),
    ] },
  ]);

